        threshold: 8,
        spec: "Obtained over $100,000 by false pretenses from {V}, a state lottery, on or about {DATE}.",
        ages: 22...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "{D}, a lottery security analyst, used internal codes to find an unclaimed $2.3 million ticket, then had a relative present a ticket bought afterward on {DATE}. {DL} says the ticket was bought honestly. The prize had gone unclaimed for two weeks when {D} ran the lookup. The relative claimed it a week later, and an audit followed about sixty days after that.",
        state: [E(.digital, 3, "Access Log", "{D} looked up the winning serial."), E(.video, 3, "Store Camera", "The relative buys a ticket days late."), E(.document, 2, "Claim Form", "Signed by the relative.")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Honest", "{DL} says the ticket was bought honestly.")],
        role: "Lottery investigator",
        exam: [.direct("What did the access log show?", "\"{D}, a lottery security analyst, looked up the winning serial number of an unclaimed $2.3 million ticket using internal codes.\""), .direct("What does the store camera show?", "\"{D.his} relative buying a ticket days late, after the lookup. That ticket was then presented as the winner.\""), .direct("How was the claim made?", "\"A claim form signed by the relative. The prize had gone unclaimed for two weeks when {D.he} ran the lookup, and the claim came a week later.\""), .direct("How do lottery prizes get claimed?", "\"A winner presents the ticket and ID at a claim center within a period. Unclaimed tickets are tracked in our system by serial number.\""), .direct("What happened after that?", "\"A week later, a claimant came forward with a ticket bought at that store. The claim form was signed by {D.his} relative.\""), .direct("What did you do after the lookup showed the serial had been run internally?", "\"I pulled every query on that serial and traced the account. The only person who had looked it up before the claim was {DL}, and the log shows the time down to the minute.\""), .cross("{D} says the ticket was bought honestly, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"Yes. And the ticket's printed time was before the draw, which is a point in {D.his} favor.\""), .cross("Do analysts run lookups as part of the job?", "\"Yes. {D.He} does that for work. But this lookup was of an unclaimed prize, which is unusual.\""), .cross("Did the defendant text the relative anything?", "\"The records show {DL} sent the relative the store location. {DL} says it was a tip.\""), .cross("The ticket's printed time was before the draw, wasn't it?", "\"Yes, it printed earlier than the draw by the store's clock. We later learned that clock was off.\""), .cross("Analysts look up serials regularly, don't they?", "\"Yes, but not to track unclaimed jackpots without a case number.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Store clerk",
        defenseExam: [.direct("When was the ticket printed, according to your system?", "\"Before the draw, by the system's clock. The timestamp on the ticket comes before the draw time.\""), .direct("Is your system clock always accurate?", "\"Not always. It can drift, and we reset it from time to time. I would not swear to the minute.\""), .direct("Do you remember the relative who bought the ticket?", "\"Vaguely. A customer bought a ticket that evening. I did not note anything unusual about it.\""), .direct("What is your job?", "\"I run the register at the store that sold the ticket. We sell hundreds of tickets a day.\""), .direct("How does the system time stamp?", "\"By the terminal clock. We reset it when it drifts.\""), .direct("Do you remember the relative?", "\"A customer bought a ticket in the evening. I do not recall a name or a face.\""), .cross("If the clock was off, could the ticket have been printed after the draw?", "\"It is possible. I cannot rule it out, and I would not want to guess either way.\""), .cross("How often does the clock drift at your store?", "\"Occasionally. A few minutes either way. Larger errors are rare, but they do happen.\""), .cross("Did you see {D} in the store?", "\"No. I never saw {D}. I only served the relative.\""), .cross("The clock could have been off by days, couldn't it?", "\"It could have been off by several minutes or an hour. I do not think by days.\""), .cross("You have no record of when the ticket was actually sold, do you?", "\"Only what the terminal says.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} stole a jackpot.", "An honest ticket."),
        allocution: "\"I made a terrible choice.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} texted the relative the store location.", "The ticket's printed time was before the draw by clerk error."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you rig the lottery claim?", "\"No. I did not rig anything. The ticket was bought honestly by my relative before the draw, according to the printed time.\""), .direct("Why did you text the relative the store location?", "\"As a tip. I mentioned a lucky store, as people do. I did not tell the relative which numbers to play.\""), .direct("Why did you look up the unclaimed serial?", "\"It was part of my work. I run lookups all the time, and that was one of many.\""), .direct("What was your role?", "\"I monitored the system for irregularities in claims and unusual patterns. I run searches by serial for audit work.\""), .direct("Why did you look up the serial?", "\"It showed up on my daily list of unclaimed big tickets. I clicked to see which store sold it.\""), .direct("What did you tell your relative?", "\"That one store had a hot streak and they might try their luck. I texted the location.\""), .cross("You looked up an unclaimed $2.3 million ticket. How is that normal?", "\"It was a routine audit check. I did not use it for personal gain.\""), .cross("A week later, your relative claimed that exact prize. Isn't that suspicious?", "\"It looks that way. But the relative's ticket printed before the draw. A clerk error may explain the time.\""), .cross("You texted the store's location. Wasn't that a way to point the relative to the ticket?", "\"It was a tip, nothing more. I did not know the relative would win.\""), .cross("You are not allowed to use inside data for your own benefit, are you?", "\"No. I did not think a tip about a store counted.\""), .cross("Your relative bought the ticket days after the draw, didn't they?", "\"That is what the video shows. The ticket printed earlier because the store's clock was wrong.\"")],
        openings: ("{D}, a lottery security analyst, used internal codes to look up an unclaimed $2.3 million ticket. A week later, {D}'s relative presented a ticket bought days after the draw, and claimed the prize.", "The defense witnesses will explain that the relative's ticket printed before the draw according to the store's system, and that {D}'s lookup was routine work."),
        closings: ("A security analyst looks up an unclaimed winner, a relative buys a ticket days late, and the prize is claimed. The access log and the camera tell the story.", "A ticket printed before the draw by the store's own clock, and a lookup that was part of the job. The State must prove the ticket was bought after, and a clock may be wrong."),
        timeline: [(-14, "The prize goes unclaimed."), (0, "{D} runs the lookup."), (7, "The relative claims."), (60, "The audit.")],
        identified: "An access log.",
        aggravating: ["The defendant took advantage of a position of trust or confidence", "The offense was committed for pecuniary gain"],
        mood: .wantsConviction,
        doctrines: ["mistakeOfFact"],
        genders: ["D": .male])

    static let mugshotRemovalFees = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.mugshotRemovalFees",
        category: .whiteCollar,
        headlines: ["Website Owner Posted Mugshots and Charged $400 to Take Them Down", "Arrest Photos for Sale"],
        law: "extortion",
        threshold: 7,
        spec: "Threatened to continue publication of {V}'s arrest photo unless paid $400, on {DATE}.",
        ages: 22...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "{D} ran a website that posted arrest photos and demanded $400 from {V} on {DATE} to remove one, even after charges were dismissed. {DL} says it was a lawful service. The photo was posted thirty days before the demand. The emails say 'pay $400 or it stays up,' and the charges against {V} had been dismissed.",
        state: [E(.digital, 3, "Emails", "'Pay $400 or it stays up.'"), E(.document, 3, "Dismissal Order", "Charges against {V} were dismissed."), E(.digital, 2, "Payment Records", "Hundreds of $400 payments.")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Service", "{DL} says it was a lawful service.")],
        role: "Mugshot subject",
        exam: [.direct("What happened after your charges were dismissed?", "\"My arrest photo was still posted on {D}'s website. The photo had been posted thirty days before the demand.\""), .direct("What did {D} demand?", "\"An email said, 'Pay $400 or it stays up.' The dismissal order was already in place when I got that email.\""), .direct("How did you feel about the posting?", "\"Humiliated. The charges were dismissed, but the photo still appeared next to my name for anyone to find.\""), .direct("What were the charges you faced?", "\"A misdemeanor from a bar dispute that was dismissed at the first hearing. I had been booked and photographed before that.\""), .direct("How did you find the photo?", "\"A friend sent me a link after searching my name online. It was on {D.his} site, with my booking number and charge listed.\""), .direct("What did the defendant say in the email?", "\"'Pay $400 or it stays up.' There was a payment link. I reread it three times.\""), .cross("Isn't a booking photo a public record?", "\"It can be, yes. The photo came from a public booking site, and I understand that.\""), .cross("Did you pay the fee?", "\"No. I did not pay. I felt it was extortion.\""), .cross("Did the defendant remove the photo eventually?", "\"Not that I know of. It stayed up for a while, and that is part of the harm.\""), .cross("Booking photos are public, aren't they?", "\"Often, yes. But my charges were dismissed, and I did not want my face tied to them.\""), .cross("You never paid the fee, did you?", "\"No. I thought it was blackmail and went to the sheriff.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Web hosting clerk",
        defenseExam: [.direct("Are booking photos usually public records?", "\"Often, yes. Many counties post them, and websites collect them from those public sources.\""), .direct("Do sites charge fees for removing photos?", "\"Some do. It is common, though I do not know exactly how {D}'s site handled it.\""), .direct("Are such fees clearly legal?", "\"That is unclear. Laws on this vary, and I am not certain where this one falls.\""), .direct("What is your job?", "\"I process hosting orders and domain registrations at a small web hosting company. {DF} has been a customer for a few years.\""), .direct("What kind of site does {D} run?", "\"A directory of public booking records. It shows photos and basic details from county sites.\""), .direct("Have you seen complaints?", "\"A few about removals. We forward them to {DF}.\""), .cross("Did you work for the defendant's site?", "\"No. I only host websites. I do not know the details of how {D.he} ran things.\""), .cross("Did the defendant ever tell you about demands for $400?", "\"No. I did not hear about demands. I only handled the hosting.\""), .cross("You do not know how the defendant sets fees, do you?", "\"No. We only handle hosting.\""), .cross("You would not pay a fee to remove your photo, would you?", "\"I would be upset. It seems unfair, especially if charges were dismissed.\""), .cross("You would agree that charging $400 and threatening to keep a photo up is a demand, wouldn't you?", "\"I would call that a harsh way to run a fee schedule. I am not defending every email, only that the photos themselves come from public booking pages.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} extorted people over mugshots.", "A lawful service."),
        allocution: "\"I thought it was legal.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} ignored takedown requests.", "The photos came from public booking sites."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you threaten {V}?", "\"No. I did not threaten anyone. I offered a removal service, and the fee was for that service.\""), .direct("Why did you charge $400?", "\"It was a service fee. Removing photos takes time, and it is how the site covers its costs.\""), .direct("Did you ignore takedown requests?", "\"Some. I did not answer every request. The volume was high, and I did not respond to all.\""), .direct("How does your site work?", "\"I collect public booking photos from county websites and post them with names and charges. People can request removal for a service fee.\""), .direct("How did you set the fee?", "\"$400 covers processing, verifying, and deleting the page, including from search engines. It takes work to do all of that.\""), .direct("Why did you ignore some requests?", "\"I got hundreds a week and could not respond to each. I fell behind.\""), .cross("The email said 'Pay $400 or it stays up.' How is that not a threat?", "\"It was a plain statement of the service. I did not mean to threaten anyone.\""), .cross("The charges against {V} were dismissed. Did that matter?", "\"It should have. I should have removed it. I did not look at each case.\""), .cross("Hundreds of people paid. Isn't that a lot of pressure?", "\"Many chose the service. I understand it feels like pressure.\""), .cross("You posted photos of people whose charges were dismissed, didn't you?", "\"Yes. I did not keep track of outcomes, and I know that was careless.\""), .cross("Hundreds of people paid $400, didn't they?", "\"Yes. I do not know how many were pressured, and I did not think of it as pressure.\"")],
        openings: ("{D} ran a website that posted arrest photos and demanded $400 from {V} to remove one, even after the charges were dismissed. The email said, 'Pay $400 or it stays up.'", "Listen for the defense evidence, which will show that booking photos are public, that {D} offered a lawful removal service, and that nothing in the email threatened harm."),
        closings: ("'Pay $400 or it stays up,' sent to a person whose charges were dismissed. Hundreds of payments show how many were pressured.", "A website using public booking photos and charging a fee to remove them. The State must prove an illegal threat, and a fee for a service is not extortion."),
        timeline: [(-30, "The photo is posted."), (0, "The demand."), (30, "{V} reports."), (90, "Charges.")],
        identified: "Emails.",
        mood: .divided,
        impact: "It cost me a job interview.",
        doctrines: ["relevance"])

    static let laneClosurePayback = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.laneClosurePayback",
        category: .publicOrder,
        headlines: ["Town Aide Ordered Bridge Lanes Closed to Punish a Mayor", "Traffic Jam on Purpose"],
        law: "obstruction",
        threshold: 7,
        spec: "Willfully obstructed justice and public duty through false orders, on {DATE}.",
        ages: 28...60,
        victim: false,
        summary: "{D}, a deputy chief of staff, ordered two bridge lanes closed on {DATE} for a fake 'traffic study' to punish a mayor. Texts show the plan. {DL} says it was a real study.",
        state: [E(.digital, 3, "Texts", "'Time for some traffic problems.'"), E(.document, 3, "Study Order", "No study was ever commissioned."), E(.statement, 2, "Traffic Engineer", "'Nobody asked for data.'")],
        defense: [E(.document, 1, "Study Memo", "A memo calling for a study.")],
        role: "Traffic engineer",
        exam: [.direct("What happened on the bridge?", "\"Two lanes were closed for what was called a traffic study. No study had been commissioned, and traffic backed up for hours.\""), .direct("What did the texts say?", "\"'Time for some traffic problems.' The texts show {D} planning the closure.\""), .direct("What did you see on the study order?", "\"No study was ever commissioned. Nobody asked for data, and I would have been the one asked.\""), .direct("What did you see on the bridge that morning?", "\"Two of four lanes were coned off at six in the morning, and traffic backed up for three miles. Commuters were stuck for over an hour.\""), .direct("What did the work order say?", "\"It listed a traffic study at the bridge, with cones and a crew, but there was no crew and no count equipment on site. Nobody had requested the data.\""), .direct("What did you tell the deputy chief's office?", "\"That no study had been scheduled and that closing lanes at rush hour needed my approval. I was told it was an order and to carry it out.\""), .cross("{D} says it was a real study, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"Yes. A real study request was filed about a year earlier. I know about it.\""), .cross("Could a closure be part of such a study?", "\"In theory, yes. But no one told me to count anything on that day.\""), .cross("Did the defendant say anything about the mayor?", "\"I heard that {D.he} said the mayor would feel it. I did not hear it directly.\""), .cross("A traffic study can involve closing lanes, can't it?", "\"In theory, with planning and notice. This one had neither, and no one was sent to count vehicles.\""), .cross("You did not hear the plan to punish the mayor yourself, did you?", "\"No. I know it from the texts that came out afterward.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Public works clerk",
        defenseExam: [.direct("Was a traffic study ever planned?", "\"Yes. Years ago, there was talk of studying that bridge, and a request was filed about a year before.\""), .direct("Was a study planned for this closure?", "\"No. Not for that day. The old request was not tied to that closure.\""), .direct("Who ordered the lane closure?", "\"{D}. The order came from {D.his} office, and we carried it out.\""), .direct("What is your role in public works?", "\"I take work orders and schedule crews and equipment for road projects. I have done it for nine years.\""), .direct("How did this order arrive?", "\"By email from {D.his} office, late at night, marked urgent. It asked for two lanes closed at dawn for a traffic study.\""), .direct("What did you do?", "\"I scheduled the cones and notified the traffic desk, since an order from that office was not one I questioned. I did not check whether a study existed.\""), .cross("Did anyone tell you it was for a study?", "\"I was told it was for a study. I did not check whether one existed.\""), .cross("Did you see any data collected?", "\"No. I did not see any counters or crews that day.\""), .cross("Did {D} seem angry at the mayor?", "\"I do not know. I only did what the order said.\""), .cross("You knew there was no study team assigned, didn't you?", "\"I noticed none was listed, but I assumed it was a separate unit's job.\""), .cross("This kind of order was unusual, wasn't it?", "\"Yes. It was unusual to close lanes at rush hour with no data crew.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} closed lanes as payback.", "A real study."),
        allocution: "\"I'm sorry for the delays.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} told a friend the mayor would 'feel it.'", "A real study request was filed a year earlier."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you close the lanes to punish the mayor?", "\"No. I ordered the closure for a real traffic study. A request for a study had been filed a year earlier.\""), .direct("What about the text, 'Time for some traffic problems'?", "\"It was a joke. I said it to a friend, and I did not mean it seriously.\""), .direct("Was the study ever filed?", "\"Yes, a year earlier. The request exists. I believed it was time to carry it out.\""), .direct("What was your job?", "\"Deputy chief of staff, handling operations for the governor's office. I often directed agencies on short notice.\""), .direct("Why did you order the lanes closed?", "\"The study request had been filed a year before, and I thought it was time to get data on bridge flow. The mayor's office had been complaining about congestion.\""), .direct("Who did you text?", "\"A friend, as a joke after a bad week. I wrote the line about traffic problems without thinking about how it would look.\""), .cross("No study was commissioned for that day, was there?", "\"Not that day. I thought one would follow. I should have set it up first.\""), .cross("You told a friend the mayor would feel it, didn't you?", "\"I said something like that. I was frustrated. It was talk.\""), .cross("You closed two lanes of a bridge with no data plan. Why?", "\"I was in a hurry. I did not plan it well.\""), .cross("The text mentions the mayor feeling it, doesn't it?", "\"Yes, and I said that out loud to the friend as well. I was frustrated by an argument over a funding refusal.\""), .cross("You did not schedule any crew to count vehicles, did you?", "\"No. I thought a crew could be added later. That was a mistake.\"")],
        openings: ("{D}, a deputy chief of staff, ordered two bridge lanes closed for a 'traffic study.' Texts say 'Time for some traffic problems,' and no study was ever commissioned.", "The defense case is that a study request was filed a year earlier and that the text was a joke between friends."),
        closings: ("Two lanes closed, no study commissioned, and a text planning traffic problems for a mayor. A real study does not need a made-up reason, and this one had none.", "An old study request and an offhand joke between friends. The State must prove the closure was payback, and a poorly planned study is not a crime."),
        timeline: [(-14, "The mayor refuses."), (0, "The order."), (0, "Gridlock."), (60, "The texts leak.")],
        identified: "Texts.",
        aggravating: ["The defendant took advantage of a position of trust or confidence"],
        mood: .wantsConviction,
        doctrines: ["burdenOfProof"])

